<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sylhet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sylhet is a city in north-eastern Bangladesh on the Surma river and the centre of Sylhet Division. Conquered by Muslim forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1303, it is known for the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Shah Jalal, and under British rule it became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> growing. In 1947 a referendum joined most of the district to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>, and many Sylhetis later emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sylhet.jpg" alt="Sylhet" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rocky Masum; This work was made by Masum-al-Hasan Rocky and released under the license(s) stated below. … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sylhet_city_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
